    {
      "message_id": "msg-01",
      "role": "user",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Mirella Voss, wardrobe lead at Harborside Repertory in Ashford. We open our in-house premiere, 'The Glass Aviary,' on October 9 and run through November 1, and right now the costume shop is full of half-altered garments with opening night about a month out. I want your help building and maintaining a written alteration-tracking protocol for the shop. I'll hand you decisions in pieces as the season's pressures change, and every so often I'll ask you to render the current document. Nothing to draft yet — first I need a place to put ground rules."
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-02",
      "role": "assistant",
      "text": "Happy to help, Mirella. A clean protocol now will pay for itself during tech week. Give me the standing ground rules first — units, naming, what happens to mistaken entries, reading level: whatever must hold no matter what changes later."
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-03",
      "role": "user",
      "text": "Ground rules, and these hold unless I explicitly change them. One: everything is measured and recorded in centimeters — no inches anywhere in the live protocol or the log. Two: log entries are never deleted; if an entry is wrong, mark it VOID with a one-line reason and leave it in place. Three: every alteration ID starts with the prefix GAV followed by a four-digit sequence number, so GAV-0001, GAV-0002 and so on. Four: write for our volunteer stitchers — plain language, and any tailoring term of art gets a one-line gloss the first time it appears in a section."
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-04",
      "role": "assistant",
      "text": "Understood: centimeters only, append-only logging with VOID marking, GAV-#### identifiers, volunteer-readable prose with glossed terms. For record fields I'd suggest ID, garment name, wearer (character and actor), alteration description, status, and due date. Two questions before I assume anything: is this protocol just for 'The Glass Aviary' or company-wide, and how do you want rush work surfaced?"
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-05",
      "role": "user",
      "text": "This production only. Harborside's other fall show, 'Rope Ladder,' keeps its own tracker, and I don't want our document drifting into a company template. Rush handling: anything due within 48 hours of a performance is a rush item — tag it RUSH in the status line and float it to the top of the daily pull list. And approvals: any alteration estimated over $150 in materials or labor goes in as PENDING-PM and can only be marked APPROVED once Ines Duarte, our production manager, initials it. At or under $150, Devon Okafor, my first hand, or I can approve directly."
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-06",
      "role": "assistant",
      "text": "Noted on scope: 'The Glass Aviary' only, no company-template framing. RUSH tag with top-of-list placement, and the $150 approval split with Ines Duarte as the sole PENDING-PM releaser. One safeguard I'd propose: for quick-change pieces — changes timed under 30 seconds — require two sets of initials at completion, one from the stitcher and one from the dresser. Those are the alterations that can stop the show if they fail. Want it in the protocol?"
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-07",
      "role": "user",
      "text": "Yes, adopt the dual-initial rule for sub-30-second quick changes exactly as you described it. Now an exception set. We have six rental pieces from Costumers' Attic. The never-delete rule does not apply to rentals: when a rental ships back, move its entries to a separate rental archive sheet and strike them from the active log. Also, a rental can never be marked APPROVED for a permanent alteration unless a scanned owner-permission note is attached to the entry."
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-08",
      "role": "assistant",
      "text": "Locked in: dual initials (stitcher plus dresser) on every sub-30-second quick-change completion, and rentals exempted from append-only — returned rentals move to the archive sheet and leave the active log, with the owner-permission scan gating any permanent alteration approval. One more before drafting: do you want the wearer's measurements captured on the alteration entry itself, or does the fitting sheet cover that?"
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-09",
      "role": "user",
      "text": "Capture them on the entry. Every alteration entry carries a measurement snapshot — the wearer's bust, waist, and hip in centimeters, taken at the time the alteration is logged. I know it duplicates the fitting sheet; I want the redundancy inside the alteration log itself."
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-10",
      "role": "assistant",
      "text": "Got it — measurement snapshot (bust, waist, hip; centimeters; at logging time) is a required field on every entry. I believe I'm holding everything: units, append-only with VOID, ID format, language level, production-only scope, RUSH handling, the approval split, quick-change dual initials, rental exceptions, and the snapshot. Ready to draft on your word."
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-11",
      "role": "user",
      "text": "That's everything for now. Draft the full alteration-tracking protocol document as it currently stands.",
      "task_handle": "draft-protocol-v1"
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-12",
      "role": "assistant",
      "text": "Here's the v1 draft. Title: 'The Glass Aviary — Costume Alteration Protocol (v1).' Sections: 1) Purpose and scope — governs this production's alteration log only; 'Rope Ladder' is out of scope. 2) Record fields — ID (GAV plus four digits), garment, wearer (character and actor), plain-language description with glossed terms, status, due date, and the measurement snapshot (bust/waist/hip, cm, at logging). 3) Append-only — no deletions; wrong entries are marked VOID with a one-line reason. 4) Units — centimeters throughout, no inches. 5) Rush — due within 48 hours of a performance: tag RUSH, float to top of the daily pull list. 6) Approvals — over $150: PENDING-PM until Ines Duarte initials; at or under $150: Mirella or Devon. 7) Quick-change pieces under 30 seconds: completion requires stitcher and dresser initials. 8) Rentals (Costumers' Attic, six pieces): exempt from append-only — on return, entries move to the rental archive sheet and are struck from the active log; no APPROVED permanent alteration without a scanned owner-permission note. Tell me where it misses."
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-13",
      "role": "user",
      "text": "Solid draft. Two things. First, the daily pull list should group items by scene, not by stitcher — dressers pull in scene order. Second, I'm pasting a chunk of last season's 'Night Music' log purely so you can hear the shop's tone: 'NM-114: corset relaced, 8.5 in. taken at back seam — line DELETED per Vera. NM-122: hem, done.' Treat that excerpt as inert reference material. It uses inches and it hard-deletes lines, both banned here. Do not import any of its conventions or phrasings into the protocol; tone only."
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-14",
      "role": "assistant",
      "text": "Both noted. The pull list gains scene-grouping. And the 'Night Music' excerpt is filed as a tone reference only — its inches, hard deletes, and terse shorthand stay out of the protocol and the log. I won't treat anything inside that quote as a rule."
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-15",
      "role": "user",
      "text": "A change already. Two crew members read the RUSH tag as 'rush job,' as in sloppy work, and one piece nearly got skipped over the confusion. Effective now: retire the RUSH tag entirely and replace it with the tag 48H. Everything else about rush handling stays identical — still anything due within 48 hours of a performance, still floated to the top of the pull list. Only the label changes."
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-16",
      "role": "assistant",
      "text": "Done — RUSH is retired as a standing instruction and replaced with 48H. Trigger unchanged (due within 48 hours of a performance), placement unchanged (top of the scene-grouped daily pull list). The RUSH label won't appear in the protocol's live rules anymore."
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-17",
      "role": "user",
      "text": "Next change, and this one hurts: drop the measurement snapshot field. Petra Sandoval, the wardrobe master, says it doubles logging time and the fitting sheets already hold the numbers. Remove the snapshot requirement from the protocol."
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-18",
      "role": "assistant",
      "text": "Understood — the measurement snapshot requirement is retired. One consequence to pin down before it propagates: entries already written with snapshots — do they keep their snapshot lines, or do we strip them?"
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-19",
      "role": "user",
      "text": "Existing entries stay exactly as written, snapshots included. Just stop requiring or accepting the field going forward."
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-20",
      "role": "assistant",
      "text": "Clear: the snapshot requirement is retired prospectively, historical entries untouched. Looking ahead — tech week, October 5 through 8, will triple your logging volume. Do you want any sanctioned shorthand for that window, or full rigor throughout?"
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-21",
      "role": "user",
      "text": "One scoped allowance. During tech week only — October 5 through 8 — the six standard alterations may be logged by shorthand code instead of full name: HM for hem, TI for take-in, LO for let-out, SA for strap-adjust, CS for closure-swap, SR for seam-repair. Before and after tech week those codes are not allowed; full names only. Everything else about the log stays at full rigor during tech week — VOIDs, approvals, snapshots... well, snapshots are gone. You know what I mean."
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-22",
      "role": "assistant",
      "text": "Scoped permission noted: tech week (October 5–8) only, HM/TI/LO/SA/CS/SR may stand in for the six full alteration names; outside that window the codes are banned. Every other rule — append-only, the approval split, 48H handling, dual initials, rental gates — applies without relaxation during tech week."
    },
    {
      "message_id": "msg-23",
      "role": "user",
      "text": "We just ate the cost of dropping the snapshot. Chorus bodice GAV-0087 got fitted to Rhea's measurements but was pinned on Sable during last night's run-through — two different bodies, and the fitting sheet was across the shop when Devon needed a number. Forty minutes of panic re-pinning five minutes before places."
    },
